<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dongzhi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dongzhi Festival, Tōji, Dongji, Tunji, Đông chí</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dongzhi is the 22nd solar term of the Chinese calendar, marking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-solstice">winter solstice</a>, and the festival that Chinese people observe at that time, around 21 to 23 December. Its origins are traced to the philosophy of yin and yang, the lengthening days representing positive energy. The festival was first celebrated during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a> (1045 BCE–256 BCE) and declared an official celebration during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> (206 BCE–220 CE), when people took a break from work, worshipped heaven and honoured their ancestors. Families still gather to eat tangyuan, balls of glutinous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> that symbolise reunion, and dumplings; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, where the day is called Dongji, porridge is made with red beans.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dongzhi.jpg" alt="Dongzhi" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alpha from Melbourne, Australia · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:汤圆_Cooked_Tang_Yuan_(329781165).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Dongzhi is the 22nd solar term of the Chinese calendar. It begins when the Sun reaches celestial longitude 270° and ends at 285°, and in common use it names the day the Sun is exactly at 270°. The festival is traced to the philosophy of yin and yang, with the lengthening days standing for yang, and its meaning is symbolised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/i-ching">I Ching</a> hexagram fu, &quot;returning&quot;. In ancient times the day was also called the &quot;festival of extreme length&quot;, since the Sun's extreme position lengthens shadows, and it was traditionally an important holiday comparable to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-new-year">Chinese New Year</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The Han people took a break from work to celebrate with their families, worshipped heaven and honoured their ancestors by burning joss paper at ancestral shrines. Those with the same surname gathered at their ancestral temples, followed by a reunion dinner. Some believed the Kitchen God went to heaven that day to report a family's conduct to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jade-emperor">Jade Emperor</a>. The festival has also been linked with the winter harvest of wheat, barley and radishes, and with special feeding of livestock.</p><p>Families eat tangyuan, balls of glutinous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> whose name sounds like the word for reunion, often served in sweet soup or with jiuniang, a mildly alcoholic rice wine. Dumplings are also eaten. Tradition credits the physician Zhang Zhongjing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> with the custom: seeing the poor suffer chilblains on their ears one cold winter, he had his apprentices make dumplings filled with lamb and other ingredients to give to them, and named the dish &quot;soup that dispels cold ears&quot;. The food is also a reminder that one is now a year older, and many elderly people count their year from Dongzhi rather than from New Year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> the day is called the &quot;Small Seol&quot;. People make a porridge of red beans, patjuk, with round sticky-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> cakes, and in the past sprinkled it on walls or doors to ward off bad ghosts. In the early Goryeo and Joseon periods those in financial difficulty settled their debts. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>, people spend the day with family and make offerings to ancestors. Taiwanese also offer nine-layer cakes of glutinous rice shaped like animals such as the chicken, duck, tortoise, pig, cow and sheep, and eat tonic foods such as mutton hot pot and ginger duck hot pot. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> many businesses let staff leave early on the day.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dongzhi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Dongzhi</b> is the 22nd solar term of the Chinese calendar and the festival observed at that time, around 21 to 23 December.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li></ul></section>
